#!/usr/bin/python3
"""olo_gpu_pages (kernel/gpu-pages), checked in KVM guests that run this
computer's own kernel (tools/dev/kvm-guest: nothing of the running system
is touched, no root):

  tools/dev/gpu-pages-check            all of it
  tools/dev/gpu-pages-check --qualify  and this kernel's build looked at: its words for kernel/gpu-pages/QUALIFIED
  tools/dev/gpu-pages-check --trial-tool   and tools/dev/gpu-pages-try itself, run in guests and stopped three ways
  tools/dev/gpu-pages-check --only-trial-tool   that alone
  tools/dev/gpu-pages-check --cost     and how long a one-page fill takes without the probe, with it, and with it in force

A guest has no GPU. The module's work is on the GPU drivers' memory pool
(TTM), which a module can fill by itself: kernel/gpu-pages/check has a
PCI driver named "xe" for QEMU's host bridge (so the guest has a device
really bound to a driver of that name, as the module asks of the device
it is told), a pool with xe's flags owned by that device or by a made-up
one of another driver's, filled while the kernel's own tracepoints are
watched on that thread, and a fixture that leaves free memory in small
pieces for certain (one page kept of every 2 MB piece it could get).
A kernel warning in a guest ends it (panic_on_warn).

- **It builds** for this kernel, with no warning, and the fixtures.
- **It takes**: it loads, having checked itself (its log line says the
  2 MB try's flags with it and as the kernel has them), beside Olo's
  other piece in the same module of the kernel (olo_gpu_memory, a live
  patch) where that is built.
- **Only xe's 2 MB try**: with it in, a pool of a driver named "xe" asks
  for 2 MB without waiting allowed and for single pages as before; a
  pool of a driver of another name is as the kernel has it; `on` written
  0 puts xe's back, 1 takes it off again; unloaded, as the kernel has
  it. `changed` counts.
- **Disarmed, it refuses**: with the kernel's probes switched off for
  everything (/sys/kernel/debug/kprobes/enabled), a probe registers and
  does nothing; the module sees what reached the page allocator and
  doesn't load.
- **In memory left in small pieces**, 64 buffers of 2 MB filled: as the
  kernel has it the thread compacts and reclaims for them (the
  fixture's own check: if it doesn't, the trial shows nothing); with
  the module, never, and every buffer is still filled.

`--qualify` then looks at the running kernel's own build, which is what
the module's check of itself can't: it reads the function's arguments
where a build keeps them. Every guest must have run this very build
(the kernel's build ID and its ttm module's, read inside each guest),
the first of them with the kernel's live patches as they are here
(Olo's own as it is in this kernel, or none: the guests in memory left
in small pieces run with none). From
the kernel's type information (/sys/kernel/btf: libbpf) and the
installed ttm module's machine code (objdump) it then says what it can
read: the function's shape; that it starts at its trace site and takes
the order from %edx, the flags from %esi and the pool from %rdi before
it writes any of them, reading the pool's own flags where the type
information has them; the direct branches to it. **That reading is a
help and not a proof**: a review fed it shapes the probe would misread
(two argument registers exchanged, an address taken and never read, a
clone of the function, a jump into its middle) and it passed them. So
it never says a build is good to list. It prints the build's words
(release, the kernel's build ID, its ttm module's, the live patch's)
and whether kernel/gpu-pages/QUALIFIED has them: listed, or *pending a
person's review* (exit status 2), which that file's own words set out.

`--trial-tool` runs the tool that tries the module on a real computer,
`tools/dev/gpu-pages-try`, as it is, in four guests (this Python and
bpftrace and journalctl carried in; only the build of a module that is
built already stood in for), with GPU
buffers being filled beside it all the while: run to its end (it must
say that it says nothing either way, a guest having no holds to show,
and leave nothing loaded; then, in the same guest, what must stop it
before it loads anything: tracing that fails, tracing with a probe
missing, a kernel warning in the phase with nothing loaded; and
another's load getting in first, which it must leave as it found it);
a kernel warning while its module is on (a plain one of the graphics
driver's own, with no word that names it a warning); a SIGTERM then; a
SIGKILL then. Each time the module must be off within
moments (how long is said) and, but for the kill, out of the kernel;
after the kill it is still in, off, the trial's own, and comes out
with `rmmod`. A review found the tool's first form going on after a
warning, calling a run with no tracing a success, and leaving its
module in and on: this is that review's test, kept.

What it does to what a person sees can only be measured on a computer
with the GPU, with its owner's word (`tools/dev/gpu-pages-try`;
kernel/TUNING.md, "Graphics memory in 2 MB pieces").
"""
